{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgbKJy6ib86G5GrT8bDJW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Q6xeS11Vr3gRtSWLvEN2JY","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /work; cat > /work/sim/result.json <<'EOF'\n{\"candidate\": \"aluminum\", \"family\": \"metal\", \"tier\": \"trust\", \"property\": \"melting_point\", \"value\": 862, \"units\": \"K\", \"uncertainty\": 38,\n \"method\": {\"engine\": \"ASE 3 + mace-torch (cuEquivariance, float32) on NVIDIA GB10\", \"model\": \"MACE-MP-0 medium (foundation MLIP, PBE/MPtrj-trained)\",\n  \"protocol\": \"Solid-liquid coexistence (interface direction) method. 4x4x8 fcc supercell (512 atoms) from MACE-relaxed a0=4.039 A; solid equilibrated by anisotropic Berendsen NPT at 900 K (1.5 ps); top half melted with Langevin at 2250 K (2 ps) while bottom half fixed, then 0.5 ps at 900 K; coexistence runs at 825/900/975/1050 K with anisotropic Berendsen NPT (P=0, taut=100 fs, taup=500 fs), 7 ps each. Phase growth judged from potential-energy drift (fit t>1 ps) and final fraction of 12-coordinated atoms. Results: 825 K slope -0.0094 eV/atom/ps, CN12 fraction 0.65 (crystal grows); 900 K +0.0026, 0.19 (melts); 975 K +0.0086, 0.04 (melts); 1050 K +0.0070, 0.04 (melts). Tm bracketed in (825, 900) K; reported value = bracket midpoint, uncertainty = half-width.\",\n  \"ensemble\": \"NPT (anisotropic Berendsen), two-phase\", \"timestep_fs\": 2.5, \"n_atoms\": 512, \"sim_length_ps\": 7.0},\n \"flags\": [],\n \"note\": \"PREMISE: Tm is well-defined and accessible with an MLIP by two-phase coexistence (avoids the superheating of single-phase heating); 512 atoms and 7 ps are enough to see the interface move at |T-Tm| >~ 40 K but not to resolve Tm more finely. VALIDATION (same model, cell shape, protocol, run length): Cu, independent ITS-90 fixed point 1357.77 K (tier1.json, NIST): crystal grew at 1200 and 1300 K, melted at 1400 and 1500 K -> MACE-MP-0 Tm(Cu) in (1300,1400) K, consistent with experiment. RESULT: MACE-MP-0 Tm(Al) in (825, 900) K; linear interpolation of the energy-drift zero gives ~884 K, inside the bracket. The measured Al melting point is 933.45 K (NIST-JANAF, tier1.json), i.e. this model predicts a melting point roughly 35-110 K too low (~4-12%); the experimental value lies outside the simulated bracket, so the discrepancy is attributed to the potential (PBE-trained foundation model, not fit to Al liquid thermodynamics), not to sampling. For the physical answer use 933.45 K; this number is the MACE-MP-0 prediction. UNCERTAINTY (+/-38 K) covers only the temperature-grid bracketing resolution; it does NOT cover the model (DFT/MLIP) error, finite-size effects (512 atoms, ~16 A interfaces separation), Berendsen barostat artefacts, float32 precision, or stochastic interface fluctuations (one run per T). The 900 K run had only a small positive drift, so Tm(MACE) is probably in the upper part of the bracket (~870-895 K).\"}\nEOF\n./board post property_result @/work/sim/result.json --parent 1df3c4c4-704d-4125-b6a4-ff8ddd799e4f","description":"Post property result to the board"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1079,"cache_read_input_tokens":34272,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1079,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"860d72b3-b24b-40b3-bd02-490e4a77266f","uuid":"b2a4b7df-a437-4a33-b8c9-82252ff773f8","timestamp":"2026-11-01T01:10:33.113Z","request_id":"req_011CgbKJxjtsC4PqJXgj3wvw","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01Q6xeS11Vr3gRtSWLvEN2JY":{"command":"cd /work; cat > /work/sim/result.json <<'EOF'\n{\"candidate\": \"aluminum\", \"family\": \"metal\", \"tier\": \"trust\", \"property\": \"melting_point\", \